A||||5||stick shift||Fiero has over heated and now it won't start!||||8||
Z||000000||stick shift||12-14-2000||11:20 PM||||I would appreciate any information on this subject. I purchased an 87 GT 2.8 liter 9 mo. ago and other than replacing bad water pump and other maintenance items, have had no problems until last week. On the way to work, the temp gauge started climbing until the temp light on dash came on. I immediately shut off engine and coasted into parking lot. That night the car started fine but after about 1 1/2 miles the temp gauge peged again so again I shut off engine and pushed it to a service station . I was low on coolant so after filling I thought my problems were over except for finding out where the coolant went(couldn't see any leaks). Car started fine but died before I got home. After sitting a few minutes, started up and I drove home. Next morning, started up as usual and I headed for work. After about 6 miles on highway I started to lose power and car finally quit altogether. I havent been able to start it since. Checked distributor first since I haven't replaced any parts there. Pick up coil, cap, rotor and wires didn't look too good so I replaced them. I am getting good spark. Haven't replaced plugs but I pulled one and it looks pretty good but had gas on it so I assume fuel system is working. Any suggestions for my next step?||||reg||8
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||12-14-2000||11:24 PM||||Sounds like a blown head gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tweek||12-14-2000||11:33 PM||||I had this very problem in my 87 Grand Am POS (With the 2.5l So Yes it was a completely diff engine)<BR>It would overheat in the course of about 5 in minutes..  Turned out to be a crack in the head, which is where all the coolant was going. Replaced the head and blew the motor a week later..  LOL Oh Well, Hope your prob isn't quite as severe...<P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||12-14-2000||11:52 PM||||I had a cracked head once (T-bird).  Thats where my water went.  As for not starting.  I don't think its directly related.  Does the engine turn over freely?  Does it even try to fire?||||reg||1
Z||000004||stick shift||12-15-2000||05:53 PM||||The engine does turn over.  However, after further checking, I discovered sludge about 4 inches above the full level on my dipstick.  I never experienced a blown head gasket or cracked head before so I didn't think to look there.  I guess I'll be spending the holiday season in an unheated garage.  Thanks for the moral support.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||12-15-2000||09:41 PM||||Man I hate to be the one to tell you this but if your oil is white and milky you may have ruined your engine. When coolant mixes with oil it breaks down the oil’s ability to lubricate and things start to where very rapidly when that happens. A cracked head may have spewed coolant into your crankcase. <P>If you have spark and fuel than that leaves one other thing needed to make an internal combustion engine run and that is air. Hook up a vacuum gauge and crank the engine. You should have 1-2 inches of cranking vacuum. Next do a compression test. You should have about 90-120 psi of compression with no more than 10% variance between all the cylinders. <P><BR>||||reg||1
